# PART 4 — DATA ACCESS ARCHITECTURE

## 4.1 Adapter architecture

Preserve the existing data adapter abstraction if it is suitable.

The objective is:

- InMemoryDataAdapter: development/demo/test only.
- PostgresDataAdapter: real application persistence.

The production application must use PostgresDataAdapter.

The demo adapter must never silently become the production fallback.

If DATABASE_URL is missing in production:

- Fail startup clearly.
- Do not initialize empty in-memory state.
- Do not report successful database readiness.
- Do not return fake records.

A missing database configuration must be an explicit startup error.

## 4.2 Repository design

Use domain-oriented repositories.

Suggested responsibilities:

- UserRepository.
- ClubRepository.
- MembershipRepository.
- FollowRepository.
- EventRepository.
- FormRepository.
- SubmissionRepository.
- RegistrationRepository.
- AttendanceRepository.
- ReportRepository.
- FinanceRepository.
- NotificationRepository.
- AuditRepository.
- SettingsRepository.

Repositories should not contain HTTP response logic.

Controllers should not contain raw SQL.

Domain services must own business rules.

The database adapter must own persistence implementation.

## 4.3 Transaction architecture

Implement transaction support in the persistence layer.

The following operations must use transactions:

- User provisioning.
- Club membership assignment.
- Club follow limit enforcement.
- Event registration.
- Registration capacity allocation.
- QR attendance verification.
- Event report submission.
- Financial record creation.
- Financial report approval.
- Administrative role changes.
- Audit logging for privileged mutations.

Use PostgreSQL transactions, constraints and appropriate locking.

Do not depend on frontend validation for data integrity.

