# PART 5 — DATABASE SCHEMA

Design a normalized PostgreSQL schema.

Use UUID primary keys, foreign keys, appropriate indexes, UTC timestamps and explicit constraints.

Use consistent naming conventions.

Use numeric types for financial amounts.

Never use floating-point types for monetary values.

The schema must support multiple clubs and multiple administrators without assuming one user belongs to only one club.

## TABLE 1 — users

Fields:

- id UUID primary key.
- auth_user_id UUID unique.
- email.
- normalized_email.
- display_name.
- avatar_url nullable.
- global_role.
- account_status.
- email_verified_at.
- created_at.
- updated_at.

Global roles:

- user.
- super_admin.

Account statuses:

- active.
- suspended.
- disabled.

Constraints:

- Unique normalized email.
- Unique Supabase Auth user ID.
- Valid role values.
- Valid account status.

Supabase Auth remains the password and identity provider.

Never store plaintext passwords.

## TABLE 2 — clubs

Fields:

- id.
- name.
- slug.
- description.
- logo_url nullable.
- status.
- created_at.
- updated_at.

Statuses:

- active.
- inactive.
- archived.

Enforce unique slugs.

## TABLE 3 — club_memberships

Fields:

- id.
- user_id.
- club_id.
- membership_role.
- permissions.
- status.
- assigned_by.
- created_at.
- updated_at.

Membership roles:

- admin.
- core_member.

A user may belong to multiple clubs.

A club may have multiple administrators and core members.

Enforce unique user-club membership.

Permission values must be validated against an explicit allowlist.

Possible permissions:

- manage_events.
- manage_forms.
- view_registrations.
- manage_registrations.
- scan_qr.
- submit_event_reports.
- view_financial_records.
- manage_club_settings.

